Carlow paid Borden an additional $2 million deposit for a two-month extension of the financing deadline, and began double-shifting the Clark plant, selling the extra production to Karlov International, which the Carlows had formed to export Pittsburgh Food & Beverage's beer and candy to Russia. To pay for this, the cycles of check kiting were ramped up, to the degree that with about $76 million in gross annual income, the companies had written checks worth a combined $560 million, half written in the three months Carlow tried to raise funding for the Wise purchase. Most of these checks passed between different accounts within the same bank, racking up $500,000 in overdraft fees that were paid with additional bad checks. The co-owners were found to have also written and cashed checks to themselves worth millions of dollars.
